<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boyacá Department</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Boyacá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boyacá <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> is a department of central Colombia in the Eastern Ranges of the Andes, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunja">Tunja</a>. Its high plateau was the northern heartland of the Muisca, whose towns included Hunza, Sugamuxi and Tundama, the modern Tunja, Sogamoso and Duitama. It gives its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a> of 1819, which secured the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boyaca-department.jpg" alt="Boyacá Department" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davemor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritacuba_.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Muisca were Chibchan-speaking farmers of the high basins and valleys of the Eastern Ranges of the Colombian Andes, the Altiplano Cundiboyacense of present-day Cundinamarca and <b>Boyacá</b>, at 2,500 to 2,800 metres above sea level.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a daring march over the Andes, Bolívar's army defeated the royalists at <b>Boyacá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-7">7 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Iraca, centred on Sugamuxi, the modern Sogamoso in <b>Boyacá</b>, was one of the territories of the Muisca Confederation.</blockquote><a href="/state/iraca">Iraca</a></li><li><blockquote>Tundama was a territory of the Muisca Confederation in the north of the high plateau, around modern Duitama in <b>Boyacá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tundama">Tundama</a></li><li><blockquote>Hunza, now Tunja in <b>Boyacá</b>, became the seat of the zaque, ruler of the northern Muisca territories, in the centuries before the Spanish conquest.</blockquote><a href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Zacazgo of Hunza</a></li></ul></section>
